⟦P0⟧Go Security Decisions⟦P1⟧Overview ⟦P2⟧⟦P3⟧This document includes decisions the Go Security team has made about⟦P4⟧various commonly-reported issues. It mostly serves as a reference⟦P5⟧for things we do not consider to be a vulnerability.⟦P6⟧This list is not comprehensive.⟦P7⟧General principles ⟦P8⟧⟦P9⟧Not every bug is a vulnerability.⟦P10⟧For any bug, it is almost always possible to construct⟦P11⟧a hypothetical scenario in which it causes a vulnerability.⟦P12⟧For example, imagine a bug in an image decoder which causes it to⟦P13⟧incorrectly decode some inputs. Now imagine a hypothetical system⟦P14⟧which accepts pictures of written cheques and makes bank transfers⟦P15⟧to satisfy the cheques. If this system contains a gateway which validates⟦P16⟧the request and a backend which performs the transfer, a parser⟦P17⟧differential in the image decoders used by the gateway and backend⟦P18⟧could result in the gateway passing a malicious operation on to the⟦P19⟧backend.⟦P20⟧Classifying an image misparsing bug as a vulnerability based on this⟦P21⟧scenario does not serve any useful purpose. Warning users that they⟦P22⟧are affected by a "vulnerability" of this nature is just noise,⟦P23⟧since few to no users will actually be affected. Calling this a⟦P24⟧vulnerability is detrimental to security, since it trains users to⟦P25⟧ignore reports as mostly meaningless.⟦P26⟧This scenario is also an example of why we do not assign severity⟦P27⟧labels to security issues: To the hypothetical cheques processor⟦P28⟧this may be a high-severity issue, but to most users it is not an⟦P29⟧issue at all.⟦P30⟧As a general principle, simply being able to construct a⟦P31⟧hypothetical scenario in which a bug causes a security issue⟦P32⟧is not sufficient cause for us to handle it as a security issue itself.⟦P33⟧Vulnerabilities ⟦P34⟧⟦P35⟧Remote Code Execution ⟦P36⟧⟦P37⟧A scenario which permits an attacker to execute code in a situation⟦P38⟧where code execution is not expected is a PRIVATE-track vulnerability.⟦P39⟧This supersedes all other decisions.⟦P40⟧This decision does not cover functions which are expected to execute code.⟦P41⟧Covered:⟦P42⟧A parse function which executes code on a malicious input.⟦P43⟧A malicious request causing the ⟦P44⟧ server to execute code.⟦P45⟧Not covered:⟦P46⟧The ⟦P47⟧ command runs tests. Running ⟦P48⟧ on attacker-controlled⟦P49⟧tests is not within our threat model.⟦P50⟧Panics ⟦P51⟧⟦P52⟧A panic when processing attacker-controlled input may be a vulnerability.⟦P53⟧A panic in a server expected to handle attacker-controlled requests,⟦P54⟧such as a ⟦P55⟧ server, is usually a PRIVATE-track vulnerability.⟦P56⟧A panic in a client, such as a ⟦P57⟧ client, is usually a PUBLIC-track⟦P58⟧vulnerability.⟦P59⟧A panic in a parse function in a package which handles plausibly⟦P60⟧malicious input, such as ⟦P61⟧ or ⟦P62⟧,⟦P63⟧is usually a PUBLIC-track vulnerablity.⟦P64⟧An input which causes a panic due to its natural size,⟦P65⟧such as a very large image, is usually not classified as a vulnerability.⟦P66⟧Invalid inputs to functions which are not clearly designed to parse⟦P67⟧potentially malicious data are not in our threat model and⟦P68⟧generally out of scope as security bugs. For example, image ⟦P69⟧parsers⟦P70⟧⟦P71⟧are expected to defend against invalid inputs, but a panic in⟦P72⟧an image ⟦P73⟧encoder⟦P74⟧ might be a bug but would not be handled⟦P75⟧as a vulnerability.⟦P76⟧A panic in a deprecated function is not a vulnerability;⟦P77⟧see the decision on ⟦P78⟧deprecated APIs⟦P79⟧ below.⟦P80⟧Excessive resource consumption ⟦P81⟧⟦P82⟧We generally treat excessive ⟦P83⟧ or memory consumption,⟦P84⟧such as a function with a runtime that is O(n²) in terms of its input size,⟦P85⟧as equivalent to a panic.⟦P86⟧Building malicious code ⟦P87⟧⟦P88⟧Building an attacker-controlled program should be safe, even when⟦P89⟧running it is not. For example, we intend it to be safe to build⟦P90⟧untrusted code which is then run in a sandbox.⟦P91⟧We strongly recommend that anyone building untrusted code consider⟦P92⟧this a defense-in-depth measure, and that builds of untrusted code⟦P93⟧also be performed in an unprivileged sandbox environment.⟦P94⟧Data exfiltation is not in our threat model.⟦P95⟧Building an attacker-controlled program may produce output which⟦P96⟧contains the contents of arbitrary local files.⟦P97⟧⟦P98⟧Executing⟦P99⟧ malicious code is also not in our threat model.⟦P100⟧Running a malicious program obviously permits it to do malicious things.⟦P101⟧A vulnerability, usually ⟦P102⟧ track:⟦P103⟧A malicious module can cause "go build" to execute arbitrary code.⟦P104⟧Not handled as a security issue:⟦P105⟧"go build" produces an error containing text from an arbitrary local file.⟦P106⟧A compiled executable contains the contents from an arbitrary local file.⟦P107⟧A malicious program can corrupt the runtime's state and execute arbitrary code.⟦P108⟧A compilation bug allows obfuscating malicious code.⟦P109⟧Non-Vulnerabilities ⟦P110⟧⟦P111⟧Attacker-controlled environment ⟦P112⟧⟦P113⟧If an attack relies on the attacker having control over the environment⟦P114⟧a program runs in, it is not a vulnerability.⟦P115⟧This includes, but is not restricted to, an attacker with the ability to⟦P116⟧add programs to ⟦P117⟧ or set arbitrary environment variables.⟦P118⟧Deprecated APIs ⟦P119⟧⟦P120⟧The bar for a bug in a deprecated ⟦P121⟧ to be considered a security issue⟦P122⟧is higher than it is for a supported ⟦P123⟧.⟦P124⟧In particular, a panic or excessive resource consumption in a deprecated ⟦P125⟧⟦P126⟧is never handled as a vulnerability. Users who require security hardening⟦P127⟧should use supported APIs.⟦P128⟧image, ⟦P129⟧: Large images ⟦P130⟧⟦P131⟧Parsing a large image can allocate a large amount of memory.⟦P132⟧For example, a 65536x65536 32-bit color image requires ⟦P133⟧⟦P134⟧to store uncompressed.⟦P135⟧Many image compression formats can reduce a large, simple image⟦P136⟧to a very small file size. Decoding the small file may allocate⟦P137⟧a large amount of memory.⟦P138⟧Users parsing untrusted images should verify the image size prior⟦P139⟧to parsing, using a function such as⟦P140⟧⟦P141⟧⟦P142⟧⟦P143⟧.⟦P144⟧We do not consider it to be a vulnerability for an image parsing⟦P145⟧function to decode a large, well-compressed image.⟦P146⟧⟦P147⟧: Redirects ⟦P148⟧⟦P149⟧The ⟦P150⟧ package's ⟦P151⟧ client handles redirects.⟦P152⟧It implements security-relevant behavior in redirect handling.⟦P153⟧For example, it strips the "Authorization" header when following⟦P154⟧a redirect to a domain that is not a subdomain or exact match⟦P155⟧for the initial request's domain.⟦P156⟧Header stripping is a defense-in-depth measure,⟦P157⟧avoiding the case where a misconfigured or compromised server⟦P158⟧inadvertently forwards a client request containing sensitive headers⟦P159⟧to an untrusted destination.⟦P160⟧Failure to strip headers on redirect does not, by itself, permit⟦P161⟧an attacker to acquire credentials passed in header.⟦P162⟧However, it may be combined with other vulnerabilities⟦P163⟧(for example, a server with an open redirect vulnerability)⟦P164⟧to do so.⟦P165⟧Changing the ⟦P166⟧ client's behavior runs a high risk of breaking⟦P167⟧existing users who depend on the current behavior.⟦P168⟧For example, the client's same origin policy currently permits subdomains⟦P169⟧(a redirect from ⟦P170⟧ to ⟦P171⟧ will preserve headers),⟦P172⟧while the ⟦P173⟧ Fetch standard does not.⟦P174⟧Aligning the client with the standard may be worthwhile,⟦P175⟧but doing so in a security release is more likely to cause⟦P176⟧pain to existing users than it is to address real vulnerabilities.⟦P177⟧Since redirect sanitization is a defense-in-depth measure,⟦P178⟧and making changes to it is risky,⟦P179⟧we consider all aspects of the ⟦P180⟧ client redirects⟦P181⟧to be out of scope for the security bug process.⟦P182⟧
